//! # Resources
//!
//! A `#[resource]` value owns an external resource. Its sibling impl defines
//! `fn close(consume self)` returning unit. Scope cleanup invokes it automatically;
//! explicit close consumes the value and prevents a second cleanup.
//!
//! ```hew
//! #[resource]
//! type Connection { id: i64, }
//! impl Connection {
//!     fn close(consume self) { println(f"closed {self.id}"); }
//! }
//! fn main() {
//!     let connection = Connection { id: 7 };
//!     println("working");
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! Keep fallible completion separate: a finish, flush or commit operation returns
//! its own Result for the caller to handle. Cleanup is not a place to discard a
//! completion error. `defer` runs in LIFO order within its block; explicit process
//! exit does not run it. Resource moves and borrowed uses remain checked through
//! records, calls and supported actor boundaries.
//!
//! Ordinary data such as JSON values needs no manual close or free. Use resources
//! for external identity and lifetime, not for bookkeeping around ordinary data.
